course: dictionary
lesson: 8843
title: Shirts, T-shirts and other meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A shirt is a piece of clothing for the top of your body. It usually has sleeves, a collar and buttons down the front. Context tells you which kind of shirt is meant: «Paint stains clothes, so you wear an old shirt or apron» tells you a shirt is something you wear to keep clean, and that you can get messy in it.
explain: A T-shirt is a special kind of shirt. «Clean glasses with water and a soft cloth, not your T-shirt, because rough cloth scratches the lenses.» The sentence shows that a T-shirt is a piece of clothing, not a cloth, and that it is soft. A T-shirt has short sleeves and no buttons.
explain: Sometimes a phrase is needed, not a single word. «Keep your shirt on» does not mean taking off your clothes. Ask: what would fit in this situation? If someone is getting cross, the answer is stay calm. When the words do not match their picture, look for the meaning the phrase gives.
fact: A shirt is a piece of clothing for the top of the body, often with sleeves, a collar and buttons.
fact: A T-shirt is a soft shirt with short sleeves and no buttons.
fact: A sports shirt, also called a jersey, shows a team's colours.
fact: «Keep your shirt on» means stay calm.
fact: Shirt is a noun. Its plural is shirts.
exercise: «Paint stains clothes, so you wear an old shirt or apron.» What is a shirt in this sentence? | *A piece of clothing for the top of your body | A soft cloth for cleaning glasses | A bucket for washing brushes
exercise: Which shirt has no buttons? | A sports shirt that shows team colours | *A T-shirt with short sleeves | A shirt with a collar and buttons
exercise: Kai's friend is getting cross, so Kai says «keep your shirt on». What does it mean? | *Stay calm | Take off your shirt | Put on a bigger shirt | Wash your shirt quickly
test: What part of speech is shirt? | *Noun | Verb | Adjective | Pronoun
test: What does a sports shirt show? | Its buttons | *A team's colours | Its sleeves | Its price
exam: «Clean glasses with water and a soft cloth, not your T-shirt, because rough cloth scratches the lenses.» What does this sentence tell you about a T-shirt? | *It is a piece of clothing, not a cloth for cleaning glasses | It is rough and scratches lenses | It has buttons and a collar | It is a cloth for cleaning glasses
